ext level Gain experience abroad Overview: Gain experience abroad Internships abroad 6 Sub-items at the next level Internships abroad Overview: Internships abroad Finding an internship abroad 2 Sub-items at the next level Finding an internship abroad Overview: Finding an internship abroad Travel and safety information Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Scholarships 5 Sub-items at the next level Scholarships Overview: Scholarships Erasmus+ Traineeship Programme Career Service Scholarship PROMOS LMU Additional scholarships Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Personal stories Internships abroad Online Job Portals for International Internships Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Career Services 14 Sub-items at the next level Career Services Overview: Career Services Partner Companies Job portal 2 Sub-items at the next level Job portal Overview: Job portal Manual Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Career and networking events 10 Sub-items at the next level Career and networking events Overview: Career and networking events Career Talk Career Marathon Meet & Greet - Learn & Apply Career Trek Workshops and Seminars 8 Sub-items at the next level Workshops and Seminars Overview: Workshops and Seminars Seminars for acquiring soft skills and application-relevant knowledge Knowledge relevant to job applications and careers Subject-specific courses Microsoft Certificate AI & IT-Security Business administration and then finally weekend! Seminars for International Students Business language courses Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Q4 Internship Flash Meet & Greet LMU Karriereforum Working here & abroad: your international career in Bavaria Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Personal advice Career mentoring Offers for International Students Ambassador Program Internships abroad Cultural & sporting events Our services for employers Privacy policy career events & services User-Support for our portal Application tips for students Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene News and events Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Language version Deutsch Switch to German / Zur deutschen Sprachversion back zur vorherigen Navigationsebene Menu open Menu close You are in the following website hierarchy: Home Workspace for Students International Student Guide You are here: Opening a bank account Opening a bank account After you have found your new home, or at least a temporary local address, the next thing on your list to do should be to open a bank account. This is crucial because large payments in Germany are usually made by bank transfer, called \"Überweisung,\" not check, credit card, or cash. You’ll need the account early on to transfer your first rent payment, your security deposit ( Kaution ), and your student services fee ( Studierendenwerksbeitrag ). A regular checking account is called a Girokonto , and a savings account is called a Sparkonto . Online and telephone banking is available, and most banks have cash machines available in their entryways, accessible 24 hours a day. If you are from an EU country, in which the euro is the standard currency, you are not obliged to open a German bank account since transferring money within the eurozone is easy and can be done online. A German bank account might, however, be a good idea so you can avoid paying a fee each time you withdraw cash. What you’ll need Certificate of proof of residence ( Meldebescheinigung ) or a copy of your rental contract ( Mietvertrag ) Proof of LMU student status (either student ID card or Zulassungsbescheid (notification of university admission) Valid identity card or passport Paying bills Most students set up an automatic monthly transfer agreement ( Dauerauftrag ) to pay their rent since it’s so convenient. To pay for utilities and a landline telephone, on the other hand, you are likely to be asked to supply bank account information and sign an Einzugsermächtigung , i.e. a permission for the utility company to automatically withdraw your monthly bill payment. This is common and, like the Dauerauftrag , can save you time and unnecessary hassles. Make sure... ... the bank you choose exempts students from routine fees (most do), and that it doesn’t charge extra for using its cash machines. You should also check with your bank in your home country about exchange rates and how they handle transfers. Need to know more? The “Make it in Germany” website has a helpful section on Money and Banking . up Share To share copy Link Share on Facebook Share on Bluesky Share on LinkedIn Related links Home Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Routing Facebook Bluesky Instagram LinkedIn Ludwig-Maximilians-Universität München Geschwister-Scholl-Platz 1 D-80539 München Tel: +49 89 2180-0 Fax: +49 89 2180-2322 Related links Sitemap Privacy policy Imprint Accessibility Press Room LMU Shop LMU München © 2021 What are you looking for? main website all LMU sites More information about Google data transfer in LMU's Privacy Policy. close Schließen"
